{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims the problem is underdetermined, but this is directly refuted by the target's own counterconditions. If 'If the problem is recognized as a standard algebraic word problem with scalar unknowns, it is fully determined' is true, then the problem is not underdetermined. This is a standard algebraic word problem with scalar unknowns, as established by the premises '$12.50 together' and 'costs $10 more'. The target invokes a non-existent covering principle (a causal pricing mechanism) to reject a mathematical problem, contradicting its own admission that the standard reading yields a unique solution.","target":"0fd4d62cbad47fa37146aae4c811941d427809e70a5708f90851462150d1b2de"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's conjecture is vacuous under the problem's standard reading, a fact it explicitly admits ('If no tax is implied, this candidate is vacuous.'). The problem statement provides no basis for invoking a sales tax mechanism. Introducing an external, unmentioned variable (tax rate t) violates the covering principle of solving the system as given, rendering the target's exploration of post-tax scenarios irrelevant to the posed question.","target":"cefa8e0906a2f0e2d765f5731e0ff667c33e20c0d29896955525531ef57f7181"}]}